A||||4||WampusCat||Dads and slave cylinders||||1||
Z||000000||WampusCat||11-09-2001||05:07 PM||||I was visiting my parents 300 miles away in Alabama when my slave cyl gave completely out.  I could bleed it and after 2 or 3 pumps of the clutch pedal it was dead again.  After calling the local combination Chevy/Chrysler dealer and all 3 of the town's parts houses, the earliest I could get a new part was "'bout a week."  I was wondering how I was going to make it back to Memphis in time for work when my 72 yr. old dad says, "What you wanna waste your money on a new slave for when you probably just need to clean the gunk out of this one?"  I remove the slave and he proceeds to disassenble it with his pocket knife and scrape/sand all the built up gunk off the piston and the inside of the cyl.  He put it back together and I reinstalled it and it's worked great since Wed and all the way back to Memphis.  I guess a guy who was born in the great depression, and was a teen during WWII when parts for everything were hard to come by, just has a different perspective on car repair.||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||11-09-2001||06:42 PM||||Great save!!!||||reg||1
